# The FDE 30-Day Curriculum: A Complete Resource Companion

## TL;DR
- **The source video is Greg Isenberg's "The Startup Ideas Podcast" episode with Vas of Varick Agents** (YouTube ID `zXysLUTLjw4`); the written plan lives at `learn.varickagents.com/fde-in-30-days`, and it maps cleanly onto a canonical resource set — Anthropic's "Building effective agents," the ReAct paper, Hamel Husain & Shreya Shankar's evals work, AWS's backoff-with-jitter essay, and the Palantir/OpenAI/Anthropic FDE literature.
- This companion gives you a **resource table per day** (resource · type · why · free/paid · time), a suggested order, an **"If you only do 10 things"** list, and a **gap analysis** flagging what the plan omits (enterprise deployment/security, SOC2/HIPAA, VPC/on-prem, data connectors, RAG over customer data, latency/streaming UX, fine-tuning vs prompting, pilot scoping).
- **Bottom line:** the plan is an excellent engineering spine — follow it — but treat the final week (Days 22–30) as the differentiator. The FDE job is won on discovery, ROI framing, and executive communication, not on the agent loop; the OpenAI and Anthropic interview loops are graded heavily on customer-discovery role-plays and "explain it to a non-technical exec."

## Key Findings

**Source provenance.** The plan is Vas from Varick Agents' compression of "a year of learning" into a 30-day roadmap, framed around the loop **audit → build → evals → deployment**. The interview premise: every company can now buy the same frontier intelligence, so the advantage moves to *deployment* — and the FDE is the person who decides where intelligence belongs. Companion materials worth pulling: the Varick "FDE in 30 Days" microsite (with a worked invoice-processing run log), "The FDE Blueprint," Greg Isenberg's X thread summarizing the episode, and — most usefully — **Varick's own FDE job posting on Ashby**, which is a primary-source spec of the role ("You sit inside client operations, figure out where time and money are leaking, design the AI agents that will fix it... mapping how data flows across NetSuite, Salesforce, and the tangle of systems").

**The through-line.** Every week adds capability to the *same* agent: agent loop → two tools → guardrails → deliberate memory → audit trail → structured outputs → schema validation → checkpointed state → resume → explicit failure paths → retries + backoff → failure taxonomy → golden dataset → eval suite → cost per query → multi-agent → then the consulting layer (pain point, why-AI, architecture, iterations, evals story, economics, engineer rehearsal, VP rehearsal, case study).

**Biggest leverage.** Vas's own framing — quoted in the ByteByteGo/Profitable Founder writeups — is the thesis of the whole plan: *"There's only one way something can go right, but there's a thousand different ways something can go wrong. If you're only building for the way it goes right, you're worth nothing."* Weeks 2–3 (recovery + evals + economics) are what separate a demo from a paid system, and the final week is what wins the interview.

