## FINAL WEEK · DAYS 22–30 — Defend the system like an FDE
*Goal: by Day 30, explain the system to both an engineer and a non-technical executive. This is the highest-leverage week for interviews.*

### Day 22 — The pain point
| Resource | Type | Why | Free/Paid | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| First Round Review, **"So You Want to Hire an FDE"** | Essay | What FDEs actually do; discovery vs implementation | Free | 45 min |
| **The Mom Test** (Rob Fitzpatrick) | Book | Discovery questions that surface *real* pain, not flattery | Paid | 3 hr |
| **Varick FDE job posting** (Ashby) | Primary source | The exact expectations: shadow workflows, interview dept heads, map data flows across NetSuite/Salesforce | Free | 15 min |

**Exercise:** For your Day 6 workflow, document who does the work, what takes time, where errors occur, what it costs.

### Day 23 — Why AI belongs
Use the Varick microsite's decision framework — **deterministic software** (rules/inputs predictable) vs **an agent** (objective clear but path/inputs vary) vs **human-in-control** (material ambiguity, accountability, irreversible consequences). Vas's point: not every step deserves an LLM. Write down what stays human and where autonomy stops.

### Day 24 — Architecture
| Resource | Type | Why | Free/Paid | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Architecture Decision Records** (Michael Nygard; adr.github.io) | Method | How to document "why each component exists" | Free | 45 min |
| Anthropic Architecture Patterns whitepaper (revisit) | Whitepaper | Reference architectures to benchmark yours against | Free | 1 hr |

**Exercise:** Write an ADR-style doc: stack, tools, models, data, memory, guardrails — and why each exists.

### Day 25 — Iterations
Reuse Hamel's error-analysis method. Narrate what v1 got wrong, what failed, what changed, how metrics improved. Interviewers probe this directly ("Walk me through a complex project you owned").

### Day 26 — The evals story
Present dataset, pass rate, failure categories, thresholds, open risks — using the Varick evaluation-report format (e.g., "41/50 passed — 82%; failures: missing data ×5, wrong record ×4; rule: confidence < 0.80 escalates").

### Day 27 — Economics
| Resource | Type | Why | Free/Paid | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| a16z / enterprise-AI ROI frameworks + McKinsey "state of AI" | Essays | Sizing time saved, error reduction, risk, revenue, cost per query | Free | 1 hr |

**Exercise:** Build a one-page business case: hours saved, errors reduced, risk mitigated, revenue, cost per query.

### Day 28 — Rehearse (engineer audience)
Reuse the Anthropic engineering essays. Rehearse architecture, decisions, failures, tradeoffs. Expect a live architecture discussion and "design an AI solution for customer X" scenario.

### Day 29 — Rehearse (VP audience)
| Resource | Type | Why | Free/Paid | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Barbara Minto, Pyramid Principle / BLUF** (Untools/Management Consulted summaries; the book for depth) | Method | Answer-first, MECE executive communication; lead with conclusion then support | Free/Paid | 1 hr |

**Exercise:** Deliver the problem → outcome → evidence → risk in plain language, bottom-line-up-front. Anthropic/OpenAI loops force you to "hold technical lines under pressure" to a simulated non-technical CISO/VP.

### Day 30 — Final checkpoint
Produce a complete FDE case study for both audiences — the portfolio artifact you leave with (agent loop, tools, guardrails, memory, audit trail, structured outputs, schema validation, checkpointed state, resume, failure taxonomy, retries+backoff, golden dataset, eval suite, cost per query, architecture doc, evaluation report, business case).

