## If you only do 10 things
1. **Anthropic, "Building effective agents"** (Day 1) — the mental model everything else hangs on.
2. **ReAct paper** (Day 1) — the founding loop.
3. **Anthropic "Writing effective tools" + MCP intro** (Day 2) — tool design + the connectivity standard.
4. **Anthropic "Effective context engineering" + Chroma "Context Rot"** (Day 4) — why more context ≠ better.
5. **Hamel & Shreya "LLM Evals FAQ"** (Days 17–18) — error analysis is the single highest-leverage skill.
6. **AWS "Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter"** (Days 10, 15) — reliability engineering in one essay.
7. **Restate "What is Durable Execution?" + LangGraph persistence + Trigger.dev docs** (Days 11–12) — resume-after-failure, on your stack.
8. **Anthropic multi-agent post + Cognition "Don't Build Multi-Agents"** (Day 20) — read both; default to single-agent.
9. **Anthropic + OpenAI prompt-caching docs** (Day 19) — the highest-ROI cost lever (up to 90% off cached input).
10. **First Round "So You Want to Hire an FDE" + Minto Pyramid Principle + one first-person interview account** (Days 22, 29) — the consulting layer that wins the offer.

## Caveats
- **Comp and demand figures are interview/marketing claims.** The "$1M/year" and "roles up 800%" numbers come from Vas, coaching sites, and recruiter blogs, not audited data; treat them as directional. Levels.fyi shows senior FDE base north of $300k at frontier labs, with total comp often crossing $500k — the $1M figure is the rare top of the range.
- **The FDE title is unstandardized.** The same role appears as Forward Deployed Engineer, Applied AI Engineer, Forward Deployed Software Engineer (FDSE), Deployment Strategist, and Customer-Facing AI Engineer across Palantir, OpenAI, Anthropic, Cohere, Databricks, Sierra, Decagon, Glean, Harvey, and others; loops and expectations vary by company, team, and customer segment.
- **Some cited third-party pieces are vendor blogs** (Temporal/DBOS/Inngest/Restate on durable execution; observability vendors on tracing) and contain competitive positioning. Cross-reference the neutral Restate "What is Durable Execution?" guide and the OpenTelemetry conventions.
- **Prompt-caching prices and model names drift.** The 90%/50% discount structure is stable, but confirm exact per-model rates on the providers' official pricing pages before quoting them in a business case.
- **OpenAI Swarm is officially deprecated** in favor of the Agents SDK — use it for learning handoff patterns only, not production.
- **The plan's own framing is a strength and a limit:** it optimizes for one deep, defensible portfolio build. That's exactly right for interviews, but a real FDE engagement adds enterprise integration, security review, and stakeholder management that a solo 30-day build can only simulate — hence the gap table above.

## Summary
The Varick 30-day plan is a strong, well-sequenced spine, and its engineering weeks (1–3) are backed almost entirely by canonical primary sources — Anthropic's engineering essays, the ReAct paper, the AWS and Google SRE reliability literature, Hamel & Shreya's evals work, and the durable-execution docs for your own stack (Trigger.dev, plus Temporal/Restate/DBOS/Inngest for the mental model). Where you should over-invest is the final week: discovery (The Mom Test, First Round), ROI framing (a one-page business case), and answer-first executive communication (Minto/BLUF), rehearsed against the real interview format that OpenAI and Anthropic use — a customer-discovery role-play plus "explain it to a non-technical VP." Close the plan's gaps around enterprise security/compliance, data connectors, RAG-over-customer-data, and pilot scoping, and you'll walk into an FDE loop able to *do the job before anyone gives you the title* — which is the entire premise of the plan.